/**

@page documentation_tarch_task_fusion_strategies Task fusion strategies

\tableofcontents

Task fusion is one of the many magic knobs that can make a huge difference in 
your application performance: If you use it too aggressively, it will hamper
the speed of your code. If you don't use it properly, you'll suffer from high
overhead - notably if you employ GPUs.

## Prepare  

To be able to fuse tasks, we have to assign tasks are unique number. A very 
simple way to do this is to create a static marker. While it is possible to
hardcode a task number, it is not particularly elegant and reasonable: If 
you later combine two different applications, you have to be absolutely sure
that they do not use the same index for different tasks. It is safer to 
rely on the peano4::parallel::getTaskType() factory method to produce unique
identifiers:


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
static int residualTaskType     = peano4::parallel::getTaskType("UpdateResidualTask");
tarch::multicore::TaskWithCopyOfFunctor* updateResidualTask = new tarch::multicore::TaskWithCopyOfFunctor(
  residualTaskType,
  tarch::multicore::Task::DefaultPriority,
  updateResidualFunctor
);  
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

<div style="background-color: #cfc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
This is absolutely fine and works. However, it becomes convenient later on, if
we have a way to access this task type number, i.e. if it is not embedded 
somewhere in a function. In a proper task class, you can make it a static 
field. We'll demonstrate workarounds for this later on.
</div>

As soon as your task type is not tarch::multicore::Task::DontFuse, the task's
canFuse() routine will return true. Consequently, Peano's technical architecture
will embed the spawned tasks into a queue and try to fuse the tasks from there.


## Pick a task orchestration

The task orchestration is controlled through tarch::multicore::setOrchestration().
For every instance of a fusable task, we either insert this task into a queue
(if it is ready) or we launch a helper task of type tarch::multicore::taskfusion::LogReadyTask
which inserts the task as soon as all in-dependencies are met.

@image html src/tarch/multicore/taskfusion/multicore_task_flow-01.png

One the task is in the queue we spawn an instance of ProcessReadyTask.
When this instance's run() is called, it checks if the underlying task has 
already been completed. If so, it returns immediately. 

If not, we check if we can do a whole set fo tasks in one rush (task fusion) or 
we pick out that one relevant task and process this one, leaving all the 
other tasks in the queue. The hope is that, at one point, there are enough
tasks remaining, so another instance of ProcessReadyTask will trigger task 
fusion.

The default orchestration is determined through tarch::multicore::orchestration::createDefaultStrategy().
You can reset the strategy manually. Most extension's Python interfaces provide
a routine for this (e.g. exahype2.project.Project.set_multicore_orchestration()).

<div style="background-color: #cfc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
Before you continue, it is reasonable to enable the statistics mode of Peano 
and to track how many tasks there are, on average, in the queue whenever an
instance of ProcessReadyTask fires off, i.e. doesn't see that its underlying
task has already completed. You get a feeling of the business of the system.
</div>


## Tweak the fusion kernel

One of the purposes of task fusion - besides a reduction of overheads - is the
capability to introduce bespoke compute kernels that handle a whole set of 
tasks instead of a single one. They might even take them and ship them to a 
GPU. For this, you have to overwrite tarch::multicore::Tasks::fuse(). The 
default is just a while loop over tasks.


## Tailor the task orchestration

The default orchestration strategies do not distinguish different task types
and they are not particularly bespoke. In most cases, you will have to tweak
them to get good performance.
Below are a few natural tweaks.
To implement them, I recommend to create a new subclass of tarch::multicore::orchestration::Strategy
based upon an existing default strategy and to amend it to your needs.


An example of such a bespoke, tailored orchestration is 
mghype::MultigridDefaultMulticoreOrchestration.


### Status

Whenever tarch::multicore::taskfusion::ProcessReadyTask::run() kicks off, it 
invokes updateFuseStatistics() on the orchestration. We receive

- the task type,
- the current number of tasks in queue, and  
- the average number of tasks in there.

This allows to make statements on the load, as the stats are only updated if 
the underlying tasks is not processed yet.


### Disable tasks

Let the task return instruction.maxTasks=0 to disable the fusion completely.
In this case, the fusable tasks are spawned as native tasks. 
This reduces overhead, but has to be used carefully: 
On the one hand, tasks will not enter their fuse() operation anymore.
On the other hand, task queue stats (size of queues, average size of queues, 
process statistics) won't be updated anymore.


### Timeouts

If the average number of ready tasks in the queue is always smaller than
the minimum number of tasks we'd need to fuse properly every time we
hit the scheduling point, the host code does handle the fused tasks
too quickly or they are too cheap. That likely means that cores on the host are
underutilised, i.e. every time we enqueue a task, its ProcessReadyTask
almost immediately kicks in. 

One strategy to mitigate this is to spawn some fusable tasks as real tasks.
You leave some of the tasks you could fuse to the CPU, hence delaying
some of the ProcessReadyTask instances. If this is not possible, you can
alternatively equip the processing with a timeout: When it becomes active
and there are not enough fusable tasks, let it wait for a few milliseconds
to see if more fusable tasks drop in.


### Reduce fused batch size

If the average number of ready tasks in the queue is higher than the
max fused tasks every time a process ready task is invoked, the
handling of the fused tasks is too slow. Again, we have two options.

We could start to spawn more tasks as native tasks on the host. The CPU
will be "more busy" with work, and the fused processing will get fewer
tasks overall to process. In return, they have more time to handle their
bunch of tasks, e.g. on an accelerator.




### Distinguish different tasks types

If you have only one single type of tasks, decisions are easy. However, most
codes feature different (fusable) task types and hence want to make different
fusion decisions depending on the type. In this case, your fuse() routine 
resembles

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tarch::multicore::orchestration::Strategy::FuseInstruction ...::fuse(int taskType) {
  switch( taskType ) {
    case 0:
      return FuseInstruction(...);
    case 1:
      return FuseInstruction(...);
  }
  assertion(false);  
  return FuseInstruction(tarch::multicore::Task::Host, 0, 1);
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This is obviously bad style. It is way more elegant to use static values of 
classes rather than hard-coded constants.

If you don't know the task numbers, you have to use a solution similar to the 
one in mghype::MultigridDefaultMulticoreOrchestration:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  private:
    std::set< int >  _residualComputationTasks;
     std::set< int >  _operatorInversionTasks;
    
[...]

tarch::multicore::orchestration::Strategy::FuseInstruction mghype::MultigridDefaultMulticoreOrchestration::fuse(int taskType) {
  if (
    not _residualComputationTasks.contains(taskType)
    and
    not _operatorInversionTasks.contains(taskType)
  ) {
    if ( peano4::parallel::getClassNameOfType(taskType).find("UpdateResidualTask")!=std::string::npos ) {
      _residualComputationTasks.insert( taskType );
    }
    if ( peano4::parallel::getClassNameOfType(taskType).find("InvertTask")!=std::string::npos ) {
      _operatorInversionTasks.insert( taskType );
    }
  }
[...]
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~






         * Here's a more in-depth explanation of the rationale behind the
         * implementation:
         *
         * ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
         *  static_cast<double>(instruction.maxTasks) < averageNumberOfPendingReadyTasksPerProcessTaskExecution
         * ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
         *
         * Every time an instance of ProcessReadyTask runs and doesn't find its
         * corresponding task, it processes a set of fused tasks. However, there
         * are always way more pending tasks in the queue than we are allowed to
         * process. So even if we take the maximum number of tasks, the one we are
         * really interested in might not be among them. We enqueue too
         * aggressively into the set of fused tasks.
         *
         * ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
         *  static_cast<double>(instruction.minTasks) > averageNumberOfPendingReadyTasksPerProcessTaskExecution
         * ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
         *
         * Every time an instance of ProcessReadyTask runs and doesn't find its
         * corresponding task, it processes a set of fused tasks. However, there
         * are never enough pending tasks in the queue so we can fuse. This
         * happens if the ProcessReadyTasks are executed too quickly. They should
         * come way later than the actually insertion of the task into the
         * fusable set. Therefore, it might be convenient to map more tasks onto
         * native tasks and therefore throttle the processing of the fused tasks
         * as we keep more cores on the host busy.

     
*/
